    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "What does it mean to be human?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "For centuries, people believed that humans stood above nature rather than being part of it. Only humans, they thought, were able to make and use tools. And only humans could speak languages and communicate meaningfully."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "That was, until Jane Goodall came along."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "It was 1957. Goodall, then 23, had educated herself deeply about animals. She wanted to find a way to work with them. Three years later, with help from "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "anthropologist",
            "zh": "人类学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " Louis Leakey, Goodall set off for the Gombe forest in Africa. There, she "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "observed",
            "ref": "v-observe"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " chimpanzees up close. She "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "labelled",
            "ref": "v-label"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the animals with names instead of numbers. No researcher had done this before."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Soon, Goodall began noticing some surprising behaviours. One male, David Greybeard, broke off a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "stalk",
            "zh": "秆"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of grass to fish for insects. Later, Goodall saw other chimpanzees use tools as well. She also discovered a rich system of communication among the animals. They had different personalities, too. Some were kind, some were tough, and many were both."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "These discoveries made people rethink what it means to be human. They also drew in many other scientists to study chimpanzees and other apes. “It was after reading her books that I put on my boots and went out into the jungle,” said Catherine Crockford, a chimpanzee expert."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But Goodall didn’t just inspire new observations. She also inspired new actions. Although she loved her life in the jungle, she left Gombe in the 1980s. Forests were being cut down fast, and hunters were killing or catching apes for trade and research. Goodall realised that studying animals alone was not enough to protect them. She travelled the world almost until the last day of her life—sharing her experiences and hoping to make a difference."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Goodall also inspired new generations. In 1991, she set up the Roots & Shoots movement. Today, this youth-action programme runs in more than 60 countries. Under Goodall’s influence, millions of students have taken on the challenge of making the world a better place for humans, other animals, and the environment we share."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p9",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Goodall is "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "gone",
            "ref": "v-gone"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", but her voice lives on. On this ever-warming planet where many animals remain at risk, it’s a voice we need more than ever."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
